-- statement:
--   The theorem states that there exist a complex Hilbert space H, a von Neumann algebra M on H, a tracial vector τ for M, an integer n ≥ 2, and two n-tuples X = (X_i) and S = (S_i) of self-adjoint bounded operators in M, such that the following hold. Here a tracial vector is a unit vector Ω (the vacuum) for which ⟨Ω, AB Ω⟩ = ⟨Ω, BA Ω⟩ for all A, B in M and which is separating for M (A Ω = 0 with A in M forces A = 0); the trace is tr(A) = ⟨Ω, AΩ⟩. The pair (X, S) is a free standard noise, meaning that each S_i has the moments of a standard semicircular element, tr(S_i^k) = Catalan(k/2) for even k and 0 for odd k, and the n+1 unital subalgebras generated by the family X and by each single S_i are free with respect to tr, in the sense that every alternating product of centered elements from consecutive different subalgebras has trace zero. Moreover, the microstates free entropy χ(X) is strictly greater than −∞, and χ(X) ≤ χ*(X,S) − 1/2, and χ*(X,S) − 1/2 < +∞. Here χ is defined as a supremum over cutoffs R > 0 of an infimum over word-length bounds m and tolerances ε > 0 of the limsup in matrix size d of d⁻² log of the Lebesgue volume of Hermitian matrix n-tuples with operator norms at most R whose normalized trace moments of words of length at most m are within ε of those of X, plus (n/2) log d. The quantity χ* is (n/2) log(2πe) plus one half of the integral over s > 0 of the positive part of n/(1+s) minus the free Fisher information of the heat-perturbed tuple X + √s S, minus one half of the integral of the positive part of the opposite difference, with the Fisher information taken as an infimum over conjugate systems in the L² closure of polynomials in X, valued in [0, ∞]. The statement thus asserts a finite-entropy example in which χ(X) lies at least 1/2 below χ*(X,S).
